    "created_at": "2026-11-12T07:27:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-12T07:29:33Z",
    "body": "> Not applied yet: D1.\r\n> \r\n> D1: No test covers the same origin-domain boundary of the new check, a cross-origin form submission, or an opaque-origin initiator. sourceElement-cross-origin.sub.html only covers a cross-origin `<a>`. When both documents set document.domain to a common parent domain, the spec exposes the element, but Firefox Nightly gives null. When the target is same-origin but only it set document.domain, the spec gives null, but Firefox Nightly exposes the `<a>`. Not applied: proposed fix https://github.com/zcorpan/wpt/tree/zcorpan/spec-review/html-13090/d1 (asserts sourceElement in the cross-origin and same-origin-domain cross-window tests, and adds same-origin-but-not-same-origin-domain and opaque-origin cases; Chrome Canary and Safari TP pass all of them).\r\n> \r\n> Reviewed commits\r\n\r\n@zcorpan  thanks! What preference do you have to move forward here? At the time I raised the PR it was origin domain, but firefox seems to have implemented same origin, dofferent from PR and the other two engines. Firefox behaviour is better, but I have no preference myself.\r\n\r\nHopefully document.domain setter can go away at some point! for what its worth ladybird doesnt implement it at all and I have seen zero web compat reports from that (yet). I am strongly against implementing it myself. Wonder how far off it is from being removed these days!",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
